\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Where output-cotangent geometry lives, and what it buys.} \textbf  {(a)} For all 11 second moments of a Transformer block, the Pythagorean split of the departure from identity, $\|\tilde  {A} - I\|_F^2$, into the part a diagonal operator can represent (teal) and the part that is pure rotation (grey); 32-layer medians at step $2{,}500$ along an unpreconditioned Muon trajectory, so the split is a property of the architecture rather than of the optimizer (\Cref  {fig:structural_audit}a shows the output side is unchanged along MuonT and WT-MLP-Lag). The companion column gives each moment's share of total dense $O(d^3)$ eigendecomposition cost across all 11 moments ($66.5\%$ output, $33.5\%$ input). Two readings follow. The only two moments a diagonal can represent---the pre-SwiGLU expansion outputs \texttt  {out:gate} and \texttt  {out:up} at width $d_{\mathrm  {ff}}$---are also the two carrying $64.0\%$ of total $O(d^3)$ cost ($32.0\%$ each, or $96.2\%$ of output-side cost), whereas the five hidden-width outputs, above all the residual-stream writers \texttt  {out:o} and \texttt  {out:down} at $\sim \!1\%$, are rotational and cost $2.5\%$ together ($0.5\%$ each). The two sides are not symmetric: the input moment at that same expanded width, \texttt  {in:down}, carries another $32.0\%$ of total cost yet is only $20.3\%$ coordinate-wise here (and $43.4\%$ along MuonT), which is why the width threshold applies to the output side alone and every input factor stays dense, as in MuonT. \textbf  {(b)} What the resulting method buys at \texttt  {520M} ($d=1024$, $10.7\text  {B}$ tokens) on $32\times \text  {A100}$, measured against Muon: held-out mixture loss (filled) and 16-domain Paloma loss (open) against steady-state step-time overhead. WT-MLP-Lag reaches $2.7\times $ MuonT's held-out gain and $2.6\times $ its Paloma gain for $1.7\times $ its overhead, whereas purely diagonal output scaling (OD-All) is dominated---costlier than MuonT and worse on both metrics.}}{3}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:overview}{{1}{3}{\textbf {Where output-cotangent geometry lives, and what it buys.} \textbf {(a)} For all 11 second moments of a Transformer block, the Pythagorean split of the departure from identity, $\|\tilde {A} - I\|_F^2$, into the part a diagonal operator can represent (teal) and the part that is pure rotation (grey); 32-layer medians at step $2{,}500$ along an unpreconditioned \mname {Muon} trajectory, so the split is a property of the architecture rather than of the optimizer (\Cref {fig:structural_audit}a shows the output side is unchanged along \mname {MuonT} and \mname {WT-MLP-Lag}). The companion column gives each moment's share of total dense $O(d^3)$ eigendecomposition cost across all 11 moments ($66.5\%$ output, $33.5\%$ input). Two readings follow. The only two moments a diagonal can represent---the pre-SwiGLU expansion outputs \texttt {out:gate} and \texttt {out:up} at width $\dff $---are also the two carrying $64.0\%$ of total $O(d^3)$ cost ($32.0\%$ each, or $96.2\%$ of output-side cost), whereas the five hidden-width outputs, above all the residual-stream writers \texttt {out:o} and \texttt {out:down} at $\sim \!1\%$, are rotational and cost $2.5\%$ together ($0.5\%$ each). The two sides are not symmetric: the input moment at that same expanded width, \texttt {in:down}, carries another $32.0\%$ of total cost yet is only $20.3\%$ coordinate-wise here (and $43.4\%$ along \mname {MuonT}), which is why the width threshold applies to the output side alone and every input factor stays dense, as in \mname {MuonT}. \textbf {(b)} What the resulting method buys at \texttt {520M} ($d=1024$, $10.7\text {B}$ tokens) on $32\times \text {A100}$, measured against \mname {Muon}: held-out mixture loss (filled) and 16-domain Paloma loss (open) against steady-state step-time overhead. \mname {WT-MLP-Lag} reaches $2.7\times $ \mname {MuonT}'s held-out gain and $2.6\times $ its Paloma gain for $1.7\times $ its overhead, whereas purely diagonal output scaling (\mname {OD-All}) is dominated---costlier than \mname {MuonT} and worse on both metrics}{figure.caption.1}{}}
