\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Mechanistic verification and multi-scale (\texttt  {130M} \& \texttt  {520M}) quality--overhead Pareto frontiers.} \textbf  {(a)} Counterfactual update rotation angle $\theta (D, D_{\text  {MuonT}})$ at step $2{,}500$ holding $W_t, U_t, R_t, \hat  {R}_t$ fixed: WT-MLP leaves Frobenius step norm slightly smaller ($0.985\times $) while rotating residual writers \texttt  {o} and \texttt  {down} by $17.7^\circ $ and $17.0^\circ $ vs.\ OD-All's $4.3^\circ $ and $5.4^\circ $. \textbf  {(b)} Matched mid-training $8\times \text  {A100}$ step-time overhead against Muon and peak allocated memory at \texttt  {130M} (\texttt  {bench\_8gpu\_opt}); input-only MuonT already costs $+1.5\%$. \textbf  {(c)} Design-space Pareto frontier at \texttt  {130M} ($d=512$, $8\times \text  {A100}$), both axes measured against Muon: WT-MLP-Lag ($+6.8\%$ step time, $+11.2 \times 10^{-3}$ quality) sits on the sharp knee, strictly dominating dense (DT-All, DT-All-Lag) and partial (No2048, OuterDense) variants. \textbf  {(d)} Width-scaled Pareto frontier at \texttt  {520M} ($d=1{,}024$, $10.74\text  {B}$ tokens), also against Muon: WT-MLP-Lag reaches $+4.95 \times 10^{-3}$ late $\mathcal  {L}_{\mathrm  {sel}}$ and $+8.93 \times 10^{-3}$ 16-domain Paloma for $+8.2\%$ step time, against MuonT's $+1.86$ and $+3.49$ for $+4.8\%$, while pure-diagonal OD-All sits below the frontier (gaining less than MuonT at higher cost). Arrows trace the systems progression at fixed quality: $+15.2\%$ unchunked on $8\times \text  {A100}$, $+12.1\%$ chunked, $+8.2\%$ on $32\times \text  {A100}$.}}{22}{figure.caption.29}\protected@file@percent }
